    (v_topic_id, 'A 64-year-old female named Pamela presents to the Emergency Department triage room in severe respiratory distress. The patient has a history of congestive heart failure. Vitals are BP 202/118 mmHg, HR 132 bpm, RR 30 rpm, and SpO2 87% on room air. The patient is sitting upright, using accessory muscles, and has diffuse crackles bilaterally and jugular venous distention. Which of the following represents the most appropriate initial therapy?', '["Administer albuterol-ipratropium nebulizer and oral metoprolol 25 mg", "Intubate the patient immediately and administer intravenous loop diuretics", "Initiate non-invasive positive pressure ventilation (BiPAP/CPAP) and administer intravenous nitroglycerin", "Administer high-flow oxygen via non-rebreather mask and intravenous morphine 4 mg", "Administer intravenous nitroprusside infusion and normal saline bolus"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The patient is presenting with Acute Decompensated Heart Failure (ADHF) with acute pulmonary edema and sympathetic crash. The first-line therapeutic goals are to reduce work of breathing and decrease preload/afterload. Non-invasive positive pressure ventilation (NIPPV: CPAP or BiPAP) is highly effective: it increases intrathoracic pressure, reducing preload, and recruits collapsed alveoli, improving oxygenation. Intravenous nitroglycerin acts as a powerful venodilator to reduce preload and, at higher doses, an afterload reducer. Morphine should be avoided. (Ref: Tintinalli''s Ch. 53; Step-Up to EM Ch. 2; Case Files: EM Case 3)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 70-year-old male named William presents to the Emergency Department triage room in severe respiratory distress. The patient has a history of congestive heart failure. Vitals are BP 194/130 mmHg, HR 132 bpm, RR 30 rpm, and SpO2 85% on room air. The patient is sitting upright, using accessory muscles, and has diffuse crackles bilaterally and jugular venous distention. Which of the following represents the most appropriate initial therapy?', '["Initiate non-invasive positive pressure ventilation (BiPAP/CPAP) and administer intravenous nitroglycerin", "Administer intravenous nitroprusside infusion and normal saline bolus", "Administer albuterol-ipratropium nebulizer and oral metoprolol 25 mg", "Administer high-flow oxygen via non-rebreather mask and intravenous morphine 4 mg", "Intubate the patient immediately and administer intravenous loop diuretics"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The patient is presenting with Acute Decompensated Heart Failure (ADHF) with acute pulmonary edema and sympathetic crash. The first-line therapeutic goals are to reduce work of breathing and decrease preload/afterload. Non-invasive positive pressure ventilation (NIPPV: CPAP or BiPAP) is highly effective: it increases intrathoracic pressure, reducing preload, and recruits collapsed alveoli, improving oxygenation. Intravenous nitroglycerin acts as a powerful venodilator to reduce preload and, at higher doses, an afterload reducer. Morphine should be avoided. (Ref: Tintinalli''s Ch. 53; Step-Up to EM Ch. 2; Case Files: EM Case 3)'),
