    (v_topic_id, 'A 70-year-old female named Linda presents to the Emergency Department triage room in severe respiratory distress. The patient has a history of congestive heart failure. Vitals are BP 194/126 mmHg, HR 102 bpm, RR 22 rpm, and SpO2 87% on room air. The patient is sitting upright, using accessory muscles, and has diffuse crackles bilaterally and jugular venous distention. Which of the following represents the most appropriate initial therapy?', '["Administer high-flow oxygen via non-rebreather mask and intravenous morphine 4 mg", "Administer albuterol-ipratropium nebulizer and oral metoprolol 25 mg", "Intubate the patient immediately and administer intravenous loop diuretics", "Initiate non-invasive positive pressure ventilation (BiPAP/CPAP) and administer intravenous nitroglycerin", "Administer intravenous nitroprusside infusion and normal saline bolus"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The patient is presenting with Acute Decompensated Heart Failure (ADHF) with acute pulmonary edema and sympathetic crash. The first-line therapeutic goals are to reduce work of breathing and decrease preload/afterload. Non-invasive positive pressure ventilation (NIPPV: CPAP or BiPAP) is highly effective: it increases intrathoracic pressure, reducing preload, and recruits collapsed alveoli, improving oxygenation. Intravenous nitroglycerin acts as a powerful venodilator to reduce preload and, at higher doses, an afterload reducer. Morphine should be avoided. (Ref: Tintinalli''s Ch. 53; Step-Up to EM Ch. 2; Case Files: EM Case 3)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 58-year-old male named Kevin presents to the high-acuity area in severe respiratory distress. The patient has a history of congestive heart failure. Vitals are BP 194/122 mmHg, HR 140 bpm, RR 30 rpm, and SpO2 89% on room air. The patient is sitting upright, using accessory muscles, and has diffuse crackles bilaterally and jugular venous distention. Which of the following represents the most appropriate initial therapy?', '["Administer albuterol-ipratropium nebulizer and oral metoprolol 25 mg", "Intubate the patient immediately and administer intravenous loop diuretics", "Administer intravenous nitroprusside infusion and normal saline bolus", "Initiate non-invasive positive pressure ventilation (BiPAP/CPAP) and administer intravenous nitroglycerin", "Administer high-flow oxygen via non-rebreather mask and intravenous morphine 4 mg"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The patient is presenting with Acute Decompensated Heart Failure (ADHF) with acute pulmonary edema and sympathetic crash. The first-line therapeutic goals are to reduce work of breathing and decrease preload/afterload. Non-invasive positive pressure ventilation (NIPPV: CPAP or BiPAP) is highly effective: it increases intrathoracic pressure, reducing preload, and recruits collapsed alveoli, improving oxygenation. Intravenous nitroglycerin acts as a powerful venodilator to reduce preload and, at higher doses, an afterload reducer. Morphine should be avoided. (Ref: Tintinalli''s Ch. 53; Step-Up to EM Ch. 2; Case Files: EM Case 3)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 70-year-old female named Sharon is admitted with an acute anterior wall STEMI. The patient is now hypotensive with BP 90/50 mmHg, tachycardic at 140 bpm, and tachypneic. She has cold, clammy extremities, poor urine output, and diffuse crackles throughout both lung fields. An echocardiogram shows severe left ventricular dysfunction with an ejection fraction of 20%. Which of the following is the most appropriate first-line pharmacotherapy for this form of shock?', '["Inotropic support with intravenous Dobutamine, along with Norepinephrine if profound hypotension is present", "High-dose intravenous furosemide 80 mg push as sole therapy", "Aggressive fluid resuscitation with 2 liters of normal saline bolus", "Intravenous metoprolol 5 mg push to reduce myocardial oxygen demand", "Intravenous nitroglycerin infusion titrated to a mean arterial pressure of 65 mmHg"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The patient is in cardiogenic shock, characterized by inadequate tissue perfusion (cold, clammy extremities, poor urine output) and pulmonary congestion (crackles) in the setting of severe cardiac dysfunction. Fluid resuscitation is contraindicated as it will worsen pulmonary edema. The primary treatment includes inotropic support with Dobutamine to increase cardiac contractility and cardiac output. If profound hypotension is present (e.g., MAP < 60 mmHg), a vasopressor such as Norepinephrine should be co-administered to maintain perfusion pressure. (Ref: Tintinalli''s Ch. 53 & 12; Step-Up to EM Ch. 2; Case Files: EM Case 3)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 82-year-old male named Anthony is admitted with an acute anterior wall STEMI. The patient is now hypotensive with BP 92/52 mmHg, tachycardic at 132 bpm, and tachypneic. He has cold, clammy extremities, poor urine output, and diffuse crackles throughout both lung fields. An echocardiogram shows severe left ventricular dysfunction with an ejection fraction of 20%. Which of the following is the most appropriate first-line pharmacotherapy for this form of shock?', '["Intravenous metoprolol 5 mg push to reduce myocardial oxygen demand", "High-dose intravenous furosemide 80 mg push as sole therapy", "Intravenous nitroglycerin infusion titrated to a mean arterial pressure of 65 mmHg", "Inotropic support with intravenous Dobutamine, along with Norepinephrine if profound hypotension is present", "Aggressive fluid resuscitation with 2 liters of normal saline bolus"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The patient is in cardiogenic shock, characterized by inadequate tissue perfusion (cold, clammy extremities, poor urine output) and pulmonary congestion (crackles) in the setting of severe cardiac dysfunction. Fluid resuscitation is contraindicated as it will worsen pulmonary edema. The primary treatment includes inotropic support with Dobutamine to increase cardiac contractility and cardiac output. If profound hypotension is present (e.g., MAP < 60 mmHg), a vasopressor such as Norepinephrine should be co-administered to maintain perfusion pressure. (Ref: Tintinalli''s Ch. 53 & 12; Step-Up to EM Ch. 2; Case Files: EM Case 3)'),
