    (v_topic_id, 'A 68-year-old male named Daniel is admitted with an acute anterior wall STEMI. The patient is now hypotensive with BP 94/60 mmHg, tachycardic at 114 bpm, and tachypneic. He has cold, clammy extremities, poor urine output, and diffuse crackles throughout both lung fields. An echocardiogram shows severe left ventricular dysfunction with an ejection fraction of 20%. Which of the following is the most appropriate first-line pharmacotherapy for this form of shock?', '["Intravenous nitroglycerin infusion titrated to a mean arterial pressure of 65 mmHg", "Inotropic support with intravenous Dobutamine, along with Norepinephrine if profound hypotension is present", "High-dose intravenous furosemide 80 mg push as sole therapy", "Intravenous metoprolol 5 mg push to reduce myocardial oxygen demand", "Aggressive fluid resuscitation with 2 liters of normal saline bolus"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The patient is in cardiogenic shock, characterized by inadequate tissue perfusion (cold, clammy extremities, poor urine output) and pulmonary congestion (crackles) in the setting of severe cardiac dysfunction. Fluid resuscitation is contraindicated as it will worsen pulmonary edema. The primary treatment includes inotropic support with Dobutamine to increase cardiac contractility and cardiac output. If profound hypotension is present (e.g., MAP < 60 mmHg), a vasopressor such as Norepinephrine should be co-administered to maintain perfusion pressure. (Ref: Tintinalli''s Ch. 53 & 12; Step-Up to EM Ch. 2; Case Files: EM Case 3)'),
