    (v_topic_id, 'A 74-year-old male named Andrew is admitted with an acute anterior wall STEMI. The patient is now hypotensive with BP 86/54 mmHg, tachycardic at 114 bpm, and tachypneic. He has cold, clammy extremities, poor urine output, and diffuse crackles throughout both lung fields. An echocardiogram shows severe left ventricular dysfunction with an ejection fraction of 20%. Which of the following is the most appropriate first-line pharmacotherapy for this form of shock?', '["Inotropic support with intravenous Dobutamine, along with Norepinephrine if profound hypotension is present", "High-dose intravenous furosemide 80 mg push as sole therapy", "Intravenous metoprolol 5 mg push to reduce myocardial oxygen demand", "Aggressive fluid resuscitation with 2 liters of normal saline bolus", "Intravenous nitroglycerin infusion titrated to a mean arterial pressure of 65 mmHg"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The patient is in cardiogenic shock, characterized by inadequate tissue perfusion (cold, clammy extremities, poor urine output) and pulmonary congestion (crackles) in the setting of severe cardiac dysfunction. Fluid resuscitation is contraindicated as it will worsen pulmonary edema. The primary treatment includes inotropic support with Dobutamine to increase cardiac contractility and cardiac output. If profound hypotension is present (e.g., MAP < 60 mmHg), a vasopressor such as Norepinephrine should be co-administered to maintain perfusion pressure. (Ref: Tintinalli''s Ch. 53 & 12; Step-Up to EM Ch. 2; Case Files: EM Case 3)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 44-year-old male named James is admitted with an acute anterior wall STEMI. The patient is now hypotensive with BP 92/52 mmHg, tachycardic at 132 bpm, and tachypneic. He has cold, clammy extremities, poor urine output, and diffuse crackles throughout both lung fields. An echocardiogram shows severe left ventricular dysfunction with an ejection fraction of 20%. Which of the following is the most appropriate first-line pharmacotherapy for this form of shock?', '["Inotropic support with intravenous Dobutamine, along with Norepinephrine if profound hypotension is present", "Aggressive fluid resuscitation with 2 liters of normal saline bolus", "Intravenous metoprolol 5 mg push to reduce myocardial oxygen demand", "High-dose intravenous furosemide 80 mg push as sole therapy", "Intravenous nitroglycerin infusion titrated to a mean arterial pressure of 65 mmHg"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The patient is in cardiogenic shock, characterized by inadequate tissue perfusion (cold, clammy extremities, poor urine output) and pulmonary congestion (crackles) in the setting of severe cardiac dysfunction. Fluid resuscitation is contraindicated as it will worsen pulmonary edema. The primary treatment includes inotropic support with Dobutamine to increase cardiac contractility and cardiac output. If profound hypotension is present (e.g., MAP < 60 mmHg), a vasopressor such as Norepinephrine should be co-administered to maintain perfusion pressure. (Ref: Tintinalli''s Ch. 53 & 12; Step-Up to EM Ch. 2; Case Files: EM Case 3)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 48-year-old female named Nancy is admitted with an acute anterior wall STEMI. The patient is now hypotensive with BP 94/60 mmHg, tachycardic at 102 bpm, and tachypneic. She has cold, clammy extremities, poor urine output, and diffuse crackles throughout both lung fields. An echocardiogram shows severe left ventricular dysfunction with an ejection fraction of 20%. Which of the following is the most appropriate first-line pharmacotherapy for this form of shock?', '["High-dose intravenous furosemide 80 mg push as sole therapy", "Aggressive fluid resuscitation with 2 liters of normal saline bolus", "Intravenous metoprolol 5 mg push to reduce myocardial oxygen demand", "Inotropic support with intravenous Dobutamine, along with Norepinephrine if profound hypotension is present", "Intravenous nitroglycerin infusion titrated to a mean arterial pressure of 65 mmHg"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The patient is in cardiogenic shock, characterized by inadequate tissue perfusion (cold, clammy extremities, poor urine output) and pulmonary congestion (crackles) in the setting of severe cardiac dysfunction. Fluid resuscitation is contraindicated as it will worsen pulmonary edema. The primary treatment includes inotropic support with Dobutamine to increase cardiac contractility and cardiac output. If profound hypotension is present (e.g., MAP < 60 mmHg), a vasopressor such as Norepinephrine should be co-administered to maintain perfusion pressure. (Ref: Tintinalli''s Ch. 53 & 12; Step-Up to EM Ch. 2; Case Files: EM Case 3)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 74-year-old male named Christopher presents with sudden-onset, severe, ''tearing'' chest pain that radiates to the back. Vitals are BP 210/115 mmHg in the right arm and 175/95 mmHg in the left arm, HR 132 bpm, and RR 20 rpm. The chest radiograph shows a widened mediastinum. A CT angiogram confirms an acute Stanford Type B aortic dissection. Which of the following represents the correct immediate medical management strategy?', '["Administer a 1 liter normal saline bolus and start a norepinephrine infusion to maintain coronary perfusion pressure", "Administer intravenous heparin and activate the catheterization laboratory for emergency coronary stenting", "Administer intravenous nitroprusside immediately to rapidly lower blood pressure, followed by oral metoprolol", "Administer intravenous esmolol or labetalol FIRST to control heart rate to <60 bpm, then add a vasodilator like nitroprusside if needed to keep SBP 100\u2013120 mmHg", "Administer intravenous tissue plasminogen activator (tPA) to dissolve any occlusive thrombi in the aorta"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The management of acute aortic dissection requires immediate reduction of blood pressure and heart rate to minimize aortic wall shear stress (dP/dt). The target heart rate is <60 bpm and target systolic blood pressure is 100–120 mmHg. Heart rate must be controlled FIRST using a short-acting beta-blocker (like esmolol or labetalol). If a vasodilator (such as nitroprusside or nicardipine) is administered before beta-blockade, it will cause reflex sympathetic activation and tachycardia, which increases dP/dt and can cause dissection propagation. (Ref: Tintinalli''s Ch. 59; Step-Up to EM Ch. 2; Case Files: EM Case 4)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 74-year-old female named Dorothy presents with sudden-onset, severe, ''tearing'' chest pain that radiates to the jaw. Vitals are BP 210/115 mmHg in the right arm and 175/95 mmHg in the left arm, HR 102 bpm, and RR 14 rpm. The chest radiograph shows a widened mediastinum. A CT angiogram confirms an acute Stanford Type B aortic dissection. Which of the following represents the correct immediate medical management strategy?', '["Administer intravenous tissue plasminogen activator (tPA) to dissolve any occlusive thrombi in the aorta", "Administer intravenous esmolol or labetalol FIRST to control heart rate to <60 bpm, then add a vasodilator like nitroprusside if needed to keep SBP 100\u2013120 mmHg", "Administer intravenous heparin and activate the catheterization laboratory for emergency coronary stenting", "Administer intravenous nitroprusside immediately to rapidly lower blood pressure, followed by oral metoprolol", "Administer a 1 liter normal saline bolus and start a norepinephrine infusion to maintain coronary perfusion pressure"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The management of acute aortic dissection requires immediate reduction of blood pressure and heart rate to minimize aortic wall shear stress (dP/dt). The target heart rate is <60 bpm and target systolic blood pressure is 100–120 mmHg. Heart rate must be controlled FIRST using a short-acting beta-blocker (like esmolol or labetalol). If a vasodilator (such as nitroprusside or nicardipine) is administered before beta-blockade, it will cause reflex sympathetic activation and tachycardia, which increases dP/dt and can cause dissection propagation. (Ref: Tintinalli''s Ch. 59; Step-Up to EM Ch. 2; Case Files: EM Case 4)'),
