    (v_topic_id, 'A 30-year-old male named Stephen presents with sudden-onset, severe, ''tearing'' chest pain that radiates to the back. Vitals are BP 210/115 mmHg in the right arm and 175/95 mmHg in the left arm, HR 102 bpm, and RR 14 rpm. The chest radiograph shows a widened mediastinum. A CT angiogram confirms an acute Stanford Type B aortic dissection. Which of the following represents the correct immediate medical management strategy?', '["Administer intravenous nitroprusside immediately to rapidly lower blood pressure, followed by oral metoprolol", "Administer intravenous heparin and activate the catheterization laboratory for emergency coronary stenting", "Administer a 1 liter normal saline bolus and start a norepinephrine infusion to maintain coronary perfusion pressure", "Administer intravenous tissue plasminogen activator (tPA) to dissolve any occlusive thrombi in the aorta", "Administer intravenous esmolol or labetalol FIRST to control heart rate to <60 bpm, then add a vasodilator like nitroprusside if needed to keep SBP 100\u2013120 mmHg"]'::jsonb, 4, 'The management of acute aortic dissection requires immediate reduction of blood pressure and heart rate to minimize aortic wall shear stress (dP/dt). The target heart rate is <60 bpm and target systolic blood pressure is 100–120 mmHg. Heart rate must be controlled FIRST using a short-acting beta-blocker (like esmolol or labetalol). If a vasodilator (such as nitroprusside or nicardipine) is administered before beta-blockade, it will cause reflex sympathetic activation and tachycardia, which increases dP/dt and can cause dissection propagation. (Ref: Tintinalli''s Ch. 59; Step-Up to EM Ch. 2; Case Files: EM Case 4)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 34-year-old male named Nicholas presents with sudden-onset, severe, ''tearing'' chest pain that radiates to the left arm. Vitals are BP 210/115 mmHg in the right arm and 175/95 mmHg in the left arm, HR 140 bpm, and RR 20 rpm. The chest radiograph shows a widened mediastinum. A CT angiogram confirms an acute Stanford Type B aortic dissection. Which of the following represents the correct immediate medical management strategy?', '["Administer intravenous tissue plasminogen activator (tPA) to dissolve any occlusive thrombi in the aorta", "Administer intravenous heparin and activate the catheterization laboratory for emergency coronary stenting", "Administer intravenous esmolol or labetalol FIRST to control heart rate to <60 bpm, then add a vasodilator like nitroprusside if needed to keep SBP 100\u2013120 mmHg", "Administer intravenous nitroprusside immediately to rapidly lower blood pressure, followed by oral metoprolol", "Administer a 1 liter normal saline bolus and start a norepinephrine infusion to maintain coronary perfusion pressure"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The management of acute aortic dissection requires immediate reduction of blood pressure and heart rate to minimize aortic wall shear stress (dP/dt). The target heart rate is <60 bpm and target systolic blood pressure is 100–120 mmHg. Heart rate must be controlled FIRST using a short-acting beta-blocker (like esmolol or labetalol). If a vasodilator (such as nitroprusside or nicardipine) is administered before beta-blockade, it will cause reflex sympathetic activation and tachycardia, which increases dP/dt and can cause dissection propagation. (Ref: Tintinalli''s Ch. 59; Step-Up to EM Ch. 2; Case Files: EM Case 4)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 78-year-old male named Thomas presents with severe shortness of breath and headache. Vitals are BP 224/126 mmHg, HR 114 bpm, and RR 24 rpm. Physical exam reveals a patient in distress with diffuse rales, jugular venous distention, and 2+ bilateral lower extremity edema. The chest radiograph demonstrates pulmonary vascular congestion. What is the most appropriate management for this hypertensive emergency?', '["Sublingual nifedipine 10 mg to achieve rapid, predictable blood pressure reduction", "Intravenous vasodilators (e.g., nitroglycerin, clevidipine, or nitroprusside) to reduce afterload, alongside intravenous loop diuretics", "Intravenous hydralazine push and oral clonidine 0.2 mg to rapidly drop the blood pressure to normal limits within 30 minutes", "Discharge the patient with instructions to double their current oral antihypertensive regimen and follow up in 24 hours", "Intravenous esmolol infusion as sole therapy to achieve a target heart rate <60 bpm"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The patient is experiencing a hypertensive emergency presenting as acute heart failure/pulmonary edema. This condition requires immediate, controlled reduction of blood pressure to reduce left ventricular afterload and relieve pulmonary congestion. The preferred agents are intravenous vasodilators such as nitroglycerin or clevidipine, along with intravenous loop diuretics (furosemide). Short-acting oral calcium channel blockers (e.g., sublingual nifedipine) are contraindicated due to the risk of precipitating rapid, uncontrolled hypotension. (Ref: Tintinalli''s Ch. 57 & 53; Step-Up to EM Ch. 2; Case Files: EM Case 3 & 4)'),
