    (v_topic_id, 'A 44-year-old female named Laura presents with a severe headache, confusion, and visual blurriness. Vitals are BP 188/122 mmHg, HR 84 bpm, RR 16 rpm, and Temp 99.1°F. Physical examination reveals bilateral papilledema and mild disorientation, but no focal neurological deficits. Head CT is negative for acute hemorrhage or ischemia. What is the most appropriate strategy for blood pressure reduction in this patient?', '["Reduce Mean Arterial Pressure (MAP) by no more than 20\u201325% in the first hour using titratable intravenous medications like nicardipine, clevidipine, or labetalol", "Rapidly reduce blood pressure to <120/80 mmHg within the first 30 minutes using intravenous hydralazine push", "Initiate oral lisinopril 20 mg and discharge home with outpatient follow-up in 48 hours", "Withhold all blood pressure medications for 24 hours to prevent hypoperfusion and cerebral ischemia", "Administer sublingual nifedipine 10 mg every 15 minutes until systolic blood pressure is below 140 mmHg"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The patient is presenting with hypertensive encephalopathy, a hypertensive emergency characterized by severe headache, confusion, visual changes, and papilledema due to breakdown of cerebral autoregulation. To prevent cerebral hypoperfusion and ischemic stroke, blood pressure should be reduced in a controlled manner: lowering Mean Arterial Pressure (MAP) by no more than 20–25% in the first hour, followed by gradual reduction to 160/100–110 mmHg over the next 2–6 hours. Highly titratable intravenous medications such as nicardipine, clevidipine, or labetalol are preferred. (Ref: Tintinalli''s Ch. 57; Step-Up to EM Ch. 2; Case Files: EM Case 4)'),
