    (v_topic_id, 'A 40-year-old male named John presents with a severe headache, confusion, and visual blurriness. Vitals are BP 194/130 mmHg, HR 72 bpm, RR 16 rpm, and Temp 98.9°F. Physical examination reveals bilateral papilledema and mild disorientation, but no focal neurological deficits. Head CT is negative for acute hemorrhage or ischemia. What is the most appropriate strategy for blood pressure reduction in this patient?', '["Withhold all blood pressure medications for 24 hours to prevent hypoperfusion and cerebral ischemia", "Administer sublingual nifedipine 10 mg every 15 minutes until systolic blood pressure is below 140 mmHg", "Initiate oral lisinopril 20 mg and discharge home with outpatient follow-up in 48 hours", "Rapidly reduce blood pressure to <120/80 mmHg within the first 30 minutes using intravenous hydralazine push", "Reduce Mean Arterial Pressure (MAP) by no more than 20\u201325% in the first hour using titratable intravenous medications like nicardipine, clevidipine, or labetalol"]'::jsonb, 4, 'The patient is presenting with hypertensive encephalopathy, a hypertensive emergency characterized by severe headache, confusion, visual changes, and papilledema due to breakdown of cerebral autoregulation. To prevent cerebral hypoperfusion and ischemic stroke, blood pressure should be reduced in a controlled manner: lowering Mean Arterial Pressure (MAP) by no more than 20–25% in the first hour, followed by gradual reduction to 160/100–110 mmHg over the next 2–6 hours. Highly titratable intravenous medications such as nicardipine, clevidipine, or labetalol are preferred. (Ref: Tintinalli''s Ch. 57; Step-Up to EM Ch. 2; Case Files: EM Case 4)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 52-year-old male named Jonathan presents with a severe headache, confusion, and visual blurriness. Vitals are BP 218/114 mmHg, HR 68 bpm, RR 18 rpm, and Temp 98.2°F. Physical examination reveals bilateral papilledema and mild disorientation, but no focal neurological deficits. Head CT is negative for acute hemorrhage or ischemia. What is the most appropriate strategy for blood pressure reduction in this patient?', '["Reduce Mean Arterial Pressure (MAP) by no more than 20\u201325% in the first hour using titratable intravenous medications like nicardipine, clevidipine, or labetalol", "Rapidly reduce blood pressure to <120/80 mmHg within the first 30 minutes using intravenous hydralazine push", "Withhold all blood pressure medications for 24 hours to prevent hypoperfusion and cerebral ischemia", "Initiate oral lisinopril 20 mg and discharge home with outpatient follow-up in 48 hours", "Administer sublingual nifedipine 10 mg every 15 minutes until systolic blood pressure is below 140 mmHg"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The patient is presenting with hypertensive encephalopathy, a hypertensive emergency characterized by severe headache, confusion, visual changes, and papilledema due to breakdown of cerebral autoregulation. To prevent cerebral hypoperfusion and ischemic stroke, blood pressure should be reduced in a controlled manner: lowering Mean Arterial Pressure (MAP) by no more than 20–25% in the first hour, followed by gradual reduction to 160/100–110 mmHg over the next 2–6 hours. Highly titratable intravenous medications such as nicardipine, clevidipine, or labetalol are preferred. (Ref: Tintinalli''s Ch. 57; Step-Up to EM Ch. 2; Case Files: EM Case 4)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 44-year-old female named Kathleen presents to the Emergency Department in a severe hypertensive crisis with a blood pressure of 218/110 mmHg, heart rate of 108 bpm, severe headache, palpitations, and sweating. The patient has a known history of pheochromocytoma and admits to missing their medications. Which of the following represents the correct class of medication and sequence of administration?', '["Administer an alpha-blocker (e.g., phentolamine or phenoxybenzamine) FIRST, followed by a beta-blocker if tachycardia persists", "Administer a pure beta-blocker like propranolol as mono-therapy, as it controls both tachycardia and vasomotor tone", "Administer a beta-blocker (e.g., metoprolol or esmolol) FIRST to control the tachyarrhythmia, followed by an alpha-blocker", "Administer intravenous lisinopril immediately to avoid any adrenergic pathway drug-drug interactions", "Administer high-dose intravenous loop diuretics to promote rapid renal clearance of excess catecholamines"]'::jsonb, 0, 'In a sympathetic crisis (e.g., pheochromocytoma, cocaine/amphetamine toxicity), there is a massive release of catecholamines stimulating both alpha- and beta-receptors. The correct management is alpha-blockade first (e.g., intravenous phentolamine or oral phenoxybenzamine) to promote vasodilation and lower blood pressure, followed by beta-blockade to control tachycardia. If a beta-blocker is administered first or alone, it blocks the vasodilatory beta-2 receptors, leaving alpha-1 vasoconstrictive receptors unopposed. This unopposed alpha-stimulation can precipitate severe vasoconstriction and dangerous hypertensive crisis. (Ref: Tintinalli''s Ch. 57 & 185; Step-Up to EM Ch. 2; Case Files: EM Case 4)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 54-year-old female named Sharon presents to the Emergency Department in a severe hypertensive crisis with a blood pressure of 194/122 mmHg, heart rate of 120 bpm, severe headache, palpitations, and sweating. The patient has a known history of pheochromocytoma and admits to missing their medications. Which of the following represents the correct class of medication and sequence of administration?', '["Administer a beta-blocker (e.g., metoprolol or esmolol) FIRST to control the tachyarrhythmia, followed by an alpha-blocker", "Administer a pure beta-blocker like propranolol as mono-therapy, as it controls both tachycardia and vasomotor tone", "Administer an alpha-blocker (e.g., phentolamine or phenoxybenzamine) FIRST, followed by a beta-blocker if tachycardia persists", "Administer high-dose intravenous loop diuretics to promote rapid renal clearance of excess catecholamines", "Administer intravenous lisinopril immediately to avoid any adrenergic pathway drug-drug interactions"]'::jsonb, 2, 'In a sympathetic crisis (e.g., pheochromocytoma, cocaine/amphetamine toxicity), there is a massive release of catecholamines stimulating both alpha- and beta-receptors. The correct management is alpha-blockade first (e.g., intravenous phentolamine or oral phenoxybenzamine) to promote vasodilation and lower blood pressure, followed by beta-blockade to control tachycardia. If a beta-blocker is administered first or alone, it blocks the vasodilatory beta-2 receptors, leaving alpha-1 vasoconstrictive receptors unopposed. This unopposed alpha-stimulation can precipitate severe vasoconstriction and dangerous hypertensive crisis. (Ref: Tintinalli''s Ch. 57 & 185; Step-Up to EM Ch. 2; Case Files: EM Case 4)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 76-year-old female named Mary presents to the Emergency Department in a severe hypertensive crisis with a blood pressure of 224/122 mmHg, heart rate of 140 bpm, severe headache, palpitations, and sweating. The patient has a known history of pheochromocytoma and admits to missing their medications. Which of the following represents the correct class of medication and sequence of administration?', '["Administer a beta-blocker (e.g., metoprolol or esmolol) FIRST to control the tachyarrhythmia, followed by an alpha-blocker", "Administer an alpha-blocker (e.g., phentolamine or phenoxybenzamine) FIRST, followed by a beta-blocker if tachycardia persists", "Administer intravenous lisinopril immediately to avoid any adrenergic pathway drug-drug interactions", "Administer high-dose intravenous loop diuretics to promote rapid renal clearance of excess catecholamines", "Administer a pure beta-blocker like propranolol as mono-therapy, as it controls both tachycardia and vasomotor tone"]'::jsonb, 1, 'In a sympathetic crisis (e.g., pheochromocytoma, cocaine/amphetamine toxicity), there is a massive release of catecholamines stimulating both alpha- and beta-receptors. The correct management is alpha-blockade first (e.g., intravenous phentolamine or oral phenoxybenzamine) to promote vasodilation and lower blood pressure, followed by beta-blockade to control tachycardia. If a beta-blocker is administered first or alone, it blocks the vasodilatory beta-2 receptors, leaving alpha-1 vasoconstrictive receptors unopposed. This unopposed alpha-stimulation can precipitate severe vasoconstriction and dangerous hypertensive crisis. (Ref: Tintinalli''s Ch. 57 & 185; Step-Up to EM Ch. 2; Case Files: EM Case 4)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 64-year-old female named Sarah presents to the Emergency Department in a severe hypertensive crisis with a blood pressure of 202/130 mmHg, heart rate of 114 bpm, severe headache, palpitations, and sweating. The patient has a known history of pheochromocytoma and admits to missing their medications. Which of the following represents the correct class of medication and sequence of administration?', '["Administer high-dose intravenous loop diuretics to promote rapid renal clearance of excess catecholamines", "Administer a beta-blocker (e.g., metoprolol or esmolol) FIRST to control the tachyarrhythmia, followed by an alpha-blocker", "Administer a pure beta-blocker like propranolol as mono-therapy, as it controls both tachycardia and vasomotor tone", "Administer intravenous lisinopril immediately to avoid any adrenergic pathway drug-drug interactions", "Administer an alpha-blocker (e.g., phentolamine or phenoxybenzamine) FIRST, followed by a beta-blocker if tachycardia persists"]'::jsonb, 4, 'In a sympathetic crisis (e.g., pheochromocytoma, cocaine/amphetamine toxicity), there is a massive release of catecholamines stimulating both alpha- and beta-receptors. The correct management is alpha-blockade first (e.g., intravenous phentolamine or oral phenoxybenzamine) to promote vasodilation and lower blood pressure, followed by beta-blockade to control tachycardia. If a beta-blocker is administered first or alone, it blocks the vasodilatory beta-2 receptors, leaving alpha-1 vasoconstrictive receptors unopposed. This unopposed alpha-stimulation can precipitate severe vasoconstriction and dangerous hypertensive crisis. (Ref: Tintinalli''s Ch. 57 & 185; Step-Up to EM Ch. 2; Case Files: EM Case 4)'),
