    (v_topic_id, 'A 38-year-old female named Ashley presents to the Emergency Department in a severe hypertensive crisis with a blood pressure of 188/130 mmHg, heart rate of 120 bpm, severe headache, palpitations, and sweating. The patient has a known history of pheochromocytoma and admits to missing their medications. Which of the following represents the correct class of medication and sequence of administration?', '["Administer intravenous lisinopril immediately to avoid any adrenergic pathway drug-drug interactions", "Administer a pure beta-blocker like propranolol as mono-therapy, as it controls both tachycardia and vasomotor tone", "Administer high-dose intravenous loop diuretics to promote rapid renal clearance of excess catecholamines", "Administer an alpha-blocker (e.g., phentolamine or phenoxybenzamine) FIRST, followed by a beta-blocker if tachycardia persists", "Administer a beta-blocker (e.g., metoprolol or esmolol) FIRST to control the tachyarrhythmia, followed by an alpha-blocker"]'::jsonb, 3, 'In a sympathetic crisis (e.g., pheochromocytoma, cocaine/amphetamine toxicity), there is a massive release of catecholamines stimulating both alpha- and beta-receptors. The correct management is alpha-blockade first (e.g., intravenous phentolamine or oral phenoxybenzamine) to promote vasodilation and lower blood pressure, followed by beta-blockade to control tachycardia. If a beta-blocker is administered first or alone, it blocks the vasodilatory beta-2 receptors, leaving alpha-1 vasoconstrictive receptors unopposed. This unopposed alpha-stimulation can precipitate severe vasoconstriction and dangerous hypertensive crisis. (Ref: Tintinalli''s Ch. 57 & 185; Step-Up to EM Ch. 2; Case Files: EM Case 4)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 52-year-old male named Ryan presents to the Emergency Department in a severe hypertensive crisis with a blood pressure of 202/110 mmHg, heart rate of 102 bpm, severe headache, palpitations, and sweating. The patient has a known history of pheochromocytoma and admits to missing their medications. Which of the following represents the correct class of medication and sequence of administration?', '["Administer high-dose intravenous loop diuretics to promote rapid renal clearance of excess catecholamines", "Administer intravenous lisinopril immediately to avoid any adrenergic pathway drug-drug interactions", "Administer an alpha-blocker (e.g., phentolamine or phenoxybenzamine) FIRST, followed by a beta-blocker if tachycardia persists", "Administer a beta-blocker (e.g., metoprolol or esmolol) FIRST to control the tachyarrhythmia, followed by an alpha-blocker", "Administer a pure beta-blocker like propranolol as mono-therapy, as it controls both tachycardia and vasomotor tone"]'::jsonb, 2, 'In a sympathetic crisis (e.g., pheochromocytoma, cocaine/amphetamine toxicity), there is a massive release of catecholamines stimulating both alpha- and beta-receptors. The correct management is alpha-blockade first (e.g., intravenous phentolamine or oral phenoxybenzamine) to promote vasodilation and lower blood pressure, followed by beta-blockade to control tachycardia. If a beta-blocker is administered first or alone, it blocks the vasodilatory beta-2 receptors, leaving alpha-1 vasoconstrictive receptors unopposed. This unopposed alpha-stimulation can precipitate severe vasoconstriction and dangerous hypertensive crisis. (Ref: Tintinalli''s Ch. 57 & 185; Step-Up to EM Ch. 2; Case Files: EM Case 4)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 56-year-old female named Karen presents to the Emergency Department in a severe hypertensive crisis with a blood pressure of 182/130 mmHg, heart rate of 120 bpm, severe headache, palpitations, and sweating. The patient has a known history of pheochromocytoma and admits to missing their medications. Which of the following represents the correct class of medication and sequence of administration?', '["Administer intravenous lisinopril immediately to avoid any adrenergic pathway drug-drug interactions", "Administer a pure beta-blocker like propranolol as mono-therapy, as it controls both tachycardia and vasomotor tone", "Administer a beta-blocker (e.g., metoprolol or esmolol) FIRST to control the tachyarrhythmia, followed by an alpha-blocker", "Administer high-dose intravenous loop diuretics to promote rapid renal clearance of excess catecholamines", "Administer an alpha-blocker (e.g., phentolamine or phenoxybenzamine) FIRST, followed by a beta-blocker if tachycardia persists"]'::jsonb, 4, 'In a sympathetic crisis (e.g., pheochromocytoma, cocaine/amphetamine toxicity), there is a massive release of catecholamines stimulating both alpha- and beta-receptors. The correct management is alpha-blockade first (e.g., intravenous phentolamine or oral phenoxybenzamine) to promote vasodilation and lower blood pressure, followed by beta-blockade to control tachycardia. If a beta-blocker is administered first or alone, it blocks the vasodilatory beta-2 receptors, leaving alpha-1 vasoconstrictive receptors unopposed. This unopposed alpha-stimulation can precipitate severe vasoconstriction and dangerous hypertensive crisis. (Ref: Tintinalli''s Ch. 57 & 185; Step-Up to EM Ch. 2; Case Files: EM Case 4)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 84-year-old male named Mark presents to the Emergency Department in a severe hypertensive crisis with a blood pressure of 188/130 mmHg, heart rate of 120 bpm, severe headache, palpitations, and sweating. The patient has a known history of pheochromocytoma and admits to missing their medications. Which of the following represents the correct class of medication and sequence of administration?', '["Administer high-dose intravenous loop diuretics to promote rapid renal clearance of excess catecholamines", "Administer intravenous lisinopril immediately to avoid any adrenergic pathway drug-drug interactions", "Administer an alpha-blocker (e.g., phentolamine or phenoxybenzamine) FIRST, followed by a beta-blocker if tachycardia persists", "Administer a pure beta-blocker like propranolol as mono-therapy, as it controls both tachycardia and vasomotor tone", "Administer a beta-blocker (e.g., metoprolol or esmolol) FIRST to control the tachyarrhythmia, followed by an alpha-blocker"]'::jsonb, 2, 'In a sympathetic crisis (e.g., pheochromocytoma, cocaine/amphetamine toxicity), there is a massive release of catecholamines stimulating both alpha- and beta-receptors. The correct management is alpha-blockade first (e.g., intravenous phentolamine or oral phenoxybenzamine) to promote vasodilation and lower blood pressure, followed by beta-blockade to control tachycardia. If a beta-blocker is administered first or alone, it blocks the vasodilatory beta-2 receptors, leaving alpha-1 vasoconstrictive receptors unopposed. This unopposed alpha-stimulation can precipitate severe vasoconstriction and dangerous hypertensive crisis. (Ref: Tintinalli''s Ch. 57 & 185; Step-Up to EM Ch. 2; Case Files: EM Case 4)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 30-year-old female named Melissa presents with a 5-day history of sharp substernal chest pain. The pain is worse when lying flat in bed and is relieved when she sits upright and leans forward. Temp is 102.6°F, BP 126/78 mmHg, HR 80 bpm. A friction rub is audible at the left lower sternal border. The ECG shows diffuse, concave ST-elevation in multiple leads and PR-segment depression in lead II, along with PR-elevation in lead aVR. Which of the following is the most appropriate treatment?', '["Intravenous corticosteroids (methylprednisolone) and observation", "High-dose NSAIDs (e.g., ibuprofen or aspirin) and Colchicine", "Intravenous heparin infusion and broad-spectrum antibiotics", "Emergent coronary angiography and percutaneous coronary intervention", "Emergent pericardiocentesis at the bedside"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The clinical presentation (pleuritic chest pain worse when supine, relieved by sitting forward), presence of a pericardial friction rub, fever, and classic ECG changes (diffuse concave ST-elevation and reciprocal PR-segment changes: depression in lead II, elevation in aVR) are pathognomonic for acute pericarditis. The first-line treatment is high-dose NSAIDs (e.g., ibuprofen or aspirin) for pain and inflammation, combined with Colchicine to reduce recurrence rates. (Ref: Tintinalli''s Ch. 54; Step-Up to EM Ch. 2; Case Files: EM Case 5)'),
