    (v_topic_id, 'A 68-year-old male named James presents with a 2-day history of sharp substernal chest pain. The pain is worse when lying flat in bed and is relieved when he sits upright and leans forward. Temp is 102.6°F, BP 134/78 mmHg, HR 92 bpm. A friction rub is audible at the left lower sternal border. The ECG shows diffuse, concave ST-elevation in multiple leads and PR-segment depression in lead II, along with PR-elevation in lead aVR. Which of the following is the most appropriate treatment?', '["Emergent pericardiocentesis at the bedside", "Intravenous heparin infusion and broad-spectrum antibiotics", "Emergent coronary angiography and percutaneous coronary intervention", "High-dose NSAIDs (e.g., ibuprofen or aspirin) and Colchicine", "Intravenous corticosteroids (methylprednisolone) and observation"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The clinical presentation (pleuritic chest pain worse when supine, relieved by sitting forward), presence of a pericardial friction rub, fever, and classic ECG changes (diffuse concave ST-elevation and reciprocal PR-segment changes: depression in lead II, elevation in aVR) are pathognomonic for acute pericarditis. The first-line treatment is high-dose NSAIDs (e.g., ibuprofen or aspirin) for pain and inflammation, combined with Colchicine to reduce recurrence rates. (Ref: Tintinalli''s Ch. 54; Step-Up to EM Ch. 2; Case Files: EM Case 5)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 34-year-old male named Jeffrey presents with severe dyspnea and chest pressure. Vitals are BP 94/58 mmHg, HR 140 bpm, RR 28 rpm. On physical examination, the patient has jugular venous distension and muffled heart sounds. The systolic blood pressure drops by 15 mmHg during inspiration. The ECG demonstrates electrical alternans. Which of the following is the most appropriate immediate management?', '["Emergent bedside ultrasound-guided pericardiocentesis", "Intravenous infusion of furosemide 80 mg push to decrease intravascular volume", "Intravenous metoprolol 5 mg push to reduce myocardial oxygen consumption", "Urgent chest tube insertion (tube thoracostomy) in the 5th intercostal space", "Immediate endotracheal intubation and initiation of positive pressure ventilation"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The patient is presenting with cardiac tamponade, characterized by Beck''s triad (hypotension, jugular venous distension, and muffled heart sounds), pulsus paradoxus (systolic BP drop >10 mmHg during inspiration), and ECG findings of electrical alternans. This is a life-threatening emergency causing mechanical cardiogenic shock. The immediate treatment of choice is emergent pericardiocentesis to remove fluid and restore ventricular filling. (Ref: Tintinalli''s Ch. 54; Step-Up to EM Ch. 2; Case Files: EM Case 5; Roberts & Hedges Ch. 13)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 44-year-old male named Mark presents with severe dyspnea and chest pressure. Vitals are BP 82/52 mmHg, HR 108 bpm, RR 30 rpm. On physical examination, the patient has jugular venous distension and muffled heart sounds. The systolic blood pressure drops by 15 mmHg during inspiration. The ECG demonstrates electrical alternans. Which of the following is the most appropriate immediate management?', '["Urgent chest tube insertion (tube thoracostomy) in the 5th intercostal space", "Intravenous infusion of furosemide 80 mg push to decrease intravascular volume", "Intravenous metoprolol 5 mg push to reduce myocardial oxygen consumption", "Emergent bedside ultrasound-guided pericardiocentesis", "Immediate endotracheal intubation and initiation of positive pressure ventilation"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The patient is presenting with cardiac tamponade, characterized by Beck''s triad (hypotension, jugular venous distension, and muffled heart sounds), pulsus paradoxus (systolic BP drop >10 mmHg during inspiration), and ECG findings of electrical alternans. This is a life-threatening emergency causing mechanical cardiogenic shock. The immediate treatment of choice is emergent pericardiocentesis to remove fluid and restore ventricular filling. (Ref: Tintinalli''s Ch. 54; Step-Up to EM Ch. 2; Case Files: EM Case 5; Roberts & Hedges Ch. 13)'),
