    (v_topic_id, 'A 80-year-old female named Pamela presents with severe dyspnea and chest pressure. Vitals are BP 94/48 mmHg, HR 132 bpm, RR 22 rpm. On physical examination, the patient has jugular venous distension and muffled heart sounds. The systolic blood pressure drops by 15 mmHg during inspiration. The ECG demonstrates electrical alternans. Which of the following is the most appropriate immediate management?', '["Emergent bedside ultrasound-guided pericardiocentesis", "Urgent chest tube insertion (tube thoracostomy) in the 5th intercostal space", "Intravenous metoprolol 5 mg push to reduce myocardial oxygen consumption", "Immediate endotracheal intubation and initiation of positive pressure ventilation", "Intravenous infusion of furosemide 80 mg push to decrease intravascular volume"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The patient is presenting with cardiac tamponade, characterized by Beck''s triad (hypotension, jugular venous distension, and muffled heart sounds), pulsus paradoxus (systolic BP drop >10 mmHg during inspiration), and ECG findings of electrical alternans. This is a life-threatening emergency causing mechanical cardiogenic shock. The immediate treatment of choice is emergent pericardiocentesis to remove fluid and restore ventricular filling. (Ref: Tintinalli''s Ch. 54; Step-Up to EM Ch. 2; Case Files: EM Case 5; Roberts & Hedges Ch. 13)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 50-year-old male named Jeffrey presents with chest pain, palpitations, and progressive dyspnea over the past week. The patient reports having a flu-like illness with fever and myalgias two weeks ago. Vitals are BP 106/68 mmHg, HR 104 bpm, and RR 20 rpm. Troponin is elevated at 1.25 ng/mL, and the chest radiograph shows mild cardiomegaly. The ECG shows sinus tachycardia with non-specific T-wave inversions. What is the most likely diagnosis?', '["Acute pericarditis", "Infective endocarditis", "Acute coronary syndrome", "Myocarditis", "Dilated cardiomyopathy"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The presentation of a young or middle-aged patient with a recent viral prodrome (flu-like symptoms) who subsequently develops chest pain, palpitations, signs of heart failure (dyspnea, cardiomegaly), and elevated cardiac enzymes (troponin) is highly suspicious for myocarditis (myocardial inflammation). ECG findings are non-specific. Management is primarily supportive, focusing on treating heart failure symptoms (ACE inhibitors, beta-blockers) and monitoring for dysrhythmias. (Ref: Tintinalli''s Ch. 53 & 54; Step-Up to EM Ch. 2; Case Files: EM Case 5)'),
