    (v_topic_id, 'A 28-year-old male named Daniel presents with chest pain, palpitations, and progressive dyspnea over the past week. The patient reports having a flu-like illness with fever and myalgias two weeks ago. Vitals are BP 106/68 mmHg, HR 104 bpm, and RR 20 rpm. Troponin is elevated at 1.25 ng/mL, and the chest radiograph shows mild cardiomegaly. The ECG shows sinus tachycardia with non-specific T-wave inversions. What is the most likely diagnosis?', '["Dilated cardiomyopathy", "Acute coronary syndrome", "Acute pericarditis", "Infective endocarditis", "Myocarditis"]'::jsonb, 4, 'The presentation of a young or middle-aged patient with a recent viral prodrome (flu-like symptoms) who subsequently develops chest pain, palpitations, signs of heart failure (dyspnea, cardiomegaly), and elevated cardiac enzymes (troponin) is highly suspicious for myocarditis (myocardial inflammation). ECG findings are non-specific. Management is primarily supportive, focusing on treating heart failure symptoms (ACE inhibitors, beta-blockers) and monitoring for dysrhythmias. (Ref: Tintinalli''s Ch. 53 & 54; Step-Up to EM Ch. 2; Case Files: EM Case 5)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 32-year-old female named Dorothy presents with a fever of 100.9°F, chills, and fatigue. The patient has a history of intravenous drug use. Vitals are BP 120/82 mmHg, HR 32 bpm. Physical exam reveals a new blowing holosystolic murmur at the apex, subungual splinter hemorrhages, and Osler nodes. The ECG shows sinus bradycardia with a new first-degree AV block (PR interval 260 ms). Which of the following is the most likely cause of the ECG abnormality and the next best step?', '["Perivalvular/valve ring abscess invading the cardiac conduction system; obtain an emergent echocardiogram (preferably transesophageal) and consult cardiology/cardiac surgery", "Normal variant in intravenous drug users; reassure the patient and check blood cultures in 24 hours", "Digoxin toxicity; administer digoxin-specific antibody fragments (Digibind) immediately", "Myocardial infarction of the SA node; activate the cardiac catheterization laboratory for emergency angiography", "Lyme carditis; initiate intravenous ceftriaxone and place temporary pacing pads"]'::jsonb, 0, 'This patient presents with signs of infective endocarditis (IE) (fever, IV drug use, new murmur, peripheral stigmata). The new ECG finding of conduction system delay (first-degree AV block or bundle branch block) in a patient with IE is a classic indicator of a perivalvular abscess (valve ring abscess). The infection has extended from the valve annulus into the adjacent cardiac skeleton and conduction pathway. A transesophageal echocardiogram (TEE) is the diagnostic test of choice. (Ref: Tintinalli''s Ch. 55 & 18; Step-Up to EM Ch. 2; Case Files: EM Case 5 & 2)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 84-year-old female named Karen presents with a fever of 100.4°F, chills, and fatigue. The patient has a history of intravenous drug use. Vitals are BP 126/76 mmHg, HR 35 bpm. Physical exam reveals a new blowing holosystolic murmur at the apex, subungual splinter hemorrhages, and Osler nodes. The ECG shows sinus bradycardia with a new first-degree AV block (PR interval 340 ms). Which of the following is the most likely cause of the ECG abnormality and the next best step?', '["Normal variant in intravenous drug users; reassure the patient and check blood cultures in 24 hours", "Lyme carditis; initiate intravenous ceftriaxone and place temporary pacing pads", "Digoxin toxicity; administer digoxin-specific antibody fragments (Digibind) immediately", "Perivalvular/valve ring abscess invading the cardiac conduction system; obtain an emergent echocardiogram (preferably transesophageal) and consult cardiology/cardiac surgery", "Myocardial infarction of the SA node; activate the cardiac catheterization laboratory for emergency angiography"]'::jsonb, 3, 'This patient presents with signs of infective endocarditis (IE) (fever, IV drug use, new murmur, peripheral stigmata). The new ECG finding of conduction system delay (first-degree AV block or bundle branch block) in a patient with IE is a classic indicator of a perivalvular abscess (valve ring abscess). The infection has extended from the valve annulus into the adjacent cardiac skeleton and conduction pathway. A transesophageal echocardiogram (TEE) is the diagnostic test of choice. (Ref: Tintinalli''s Ch. 55 & 18; Step-Up to EM Ch. 2; Case Files: EM Case 5 & 2)'),
